the full expression on the right-hand side:\n\\[\n(x-3)^3 + c_2 (x-3)^2 + c_1 (x-3) + c_0 = x^3 - 9x^2 + 27x - 27 + c_2 (x^2 - 6x + 9) + c_1 (x - 3) + c_0.\n\\]\nSimplify the expression by distributing and combining like terms:\n\\[\nx^3 - 9x^2 + 27x - 27 + c_2 x^2 - 6c_2 x + 9c_2 + c_1 x - 3c_1 + c_0 = x^3 + (c_2 - 9)x^2 + (27 - 6c_2 + c_1)x + (-27 + 9c_2 - 3c_1 + c_0).\n\\]\nWe now compare the coefficients of the expanded form with the original polynomial \\(x^3 + x^2 - 5\\):\n\\[\nx^3 + (c_2 - 9)x^2 + (27 - 6c_2 + c_1)x + (-27 + 9c_2 - 3c_1 + c_0) = x^3 + x^2 - 5.\n\\]\nBy comparing the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n1. \\(c_2 - 9 = 1\\),\n2. \\(27 - 6c_2 + c_1 = 1\\),\n3. \\(-27 + 9c_2 - 3c_1 + c_0 = -5\\).\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"Continue from the interruption. Output only the continuation. Do not restart the solution.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"4eecb13e8494563460f7cf204620970d0fffb3e2d2a96835b8485dfb756495fd"},"seconds":26.27682375907898,"seed":182222686593251734,"stop":"eos","text":"From the first equation, we can solve for \\(c_2\\):\n\\[\nc_2 - 9 = 1 \\implies c_2 = 10.\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\(c_2 = 10\\) into the second equation:\n\\[\n27 - 6c_2 + c_1 = 1 \\implies 27 - 6(10) + c_1 = 1 \\implies 27 - 60 + c_1 = 1 \\implies -33 + c_1 = 1 \\implies c_1 = 34.\n\\]\n\nNow substitute \\(c_2 = 10\\) and \\(c_1 = 34\\) into the third equation:\n\\[\n-27 + 9c_2 - 3c_1 + c_0 = -5 \\implies -27 + 9(10) - 3(34) + c_0 = -5 \\implies -27 + 90 - 102 + c_0 = -5 \\implies -39 + c_0 = -5 \\implies c_0 = 34.\n\\]\n\nThus, we have \\(c_2 = 10\\), \\(c_1 = 34\\), and \\(c_0 = 34\\). To find \\(c_2^2 + c_1^2 + c_0^2\\), we calculate:\n\\[\nc_2^2 + c_1^2 + c_0^2 = 10^2 + 34^2 + 34^2 = 100 + 1156 + 1156 = 2412.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2412}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{2412}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":true},"graded_text":"From the first equation, we can solve for \\(c_2\\):\n\\[\nc_2 - 9 = 1 \\implies c_2 = 10.\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\(c_2 = 10\\) into the second equation:\n\\[\n27 - 6c_2 + c_1 = 1 \\implies 27 - 6(10) + c_1 = 1 \\implies 27 - 60 + c_1 = 1 \\implies -33 + c_1 = 1 \\implies c_1 = 34.\n\\]\n\nNow substitute \\(c_2 = 10\\) and \\(c_1 = 34\\) into the third equation:\n\\[\n-27 + 9c_2 - 3c_1 + c_0 = -5 \\implies -27 + 9(10) - 3(34) + c_0 = -5 \\implies -27 + 90 - 102 + c_0 = -5 \\implies -39 + c_0 = -5 \\implies c_0 = 34.\n\\]\n\nThus, we have \\(c_2 = 10\\), \\(c_1 = 34\\), and \\(c_0 = 34\\). To find \\(c_2^2 + c_1^2 + c_0^2\\), we calculate:\n\\[\nc_2^2 + c_1^2 + c_0^2 = 10^2 + 34^2 + 34^2 = 100 + 1156 + 1156 = 2412.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2412}.\n\\]","id":"intermediate_algebra:417"}
